WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.100
Okay.
好的。

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:04.240
I am holding a new iPhone 4S in my hand.
我手里拿的是一台全新的 iPhone 4S。

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.410
This is the one that you can talk to and it talks back.
这就是那台你可以跟它说话、它也会回应你的手机。

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.260
It's a program called Siri.
这是一个叫 Siri 的程序。

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:10.760
So let's try this.
那我们来试试。

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.720
I'm just going to hold it up to the microphone here and push a button.
我把它举到麦克风这里，然后按一个按钮。

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.440
Siri, where do you come from?
Siri，你来自哪里？

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.820
I, Siri, was designed by Apple in California.
我，Siri，是由苹果公司在加利福尼亚设计的。

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.440
Where were you manufactured?
你在哪里制造的？

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:27.780
manufactured.
制造的。

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:30.880
I'm not allowed to say.
我不被允许说。

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:34.680
Why?
为什么？

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.360
Good question.
好问题。

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.620
Anything else I can do for you?
还有什么我可以帮你吗？

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:54.960
Can I say, I love especially, I am not allowed to say, because it implies that Siri somehow, you know, knows the answer, but she's just not allowed to tell me, which is insane because Because she's a machine.
我能说，我特别想说，我不被允许说，因为这暗示了 Siri 某种程度上，你知道的，知道答案，但她就是不被允许告诉我，这太荒谬了，因为她是一台机器。

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.680
Especially because, flip over the phone, right here on the back, it's printed, assembled in China.
尤其是因为，把手机翻过来，背面就印着：中国组装。

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.920
Which, you know, we all know anyway.
其实，我们大家本来都知道。

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.240
It's not like this is some big secret.
这又不是什么惊天大秘密。

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.820
We all know that stuff is made in China.
我们都知道东西都是中国制造的。

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.000
Our phones, our computers, our clothes, our household goods.
我们的手机，我们的电脑，我们的衣服，我们的日用品。

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:21.200
A couple weeks ago, I saw this one-man show where this guy did something on stage I thought was really kind of amazing.
几个星期前，我看了一个独角戏演出，那个演员在舞台上做了一件事，我觉得真的很了不起。

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:37.420
He took this fact that we all already know, right, this fact that our stuff is made overseas in maybe not the greatest working conditions, and he made the audience actually feel something about that fact, which, you know, is really quite a trick.
他把我们大家都已经知道的事实拿出来，也就是我们的东西都是在海外制造的、工作环境可能不是最好的这个事实，然后他让观众对这个事实真正产生了一些感受，你知道，这真的是个绝招。

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.220
You really have to know how to tell a story to be able to pull something like that off.
你必须真正懂得如何讲故事，才能把这样的事情表现出来。

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.780
And I bring this up because today we are excerpting that story here on the radio show.
我提到这个，是因为今天我们将在广播节目中节选这个故事。

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:52.200
The guy's name is Mike Daisy, and he makes his living doing monologues on stage.
那个人的名字叫迈克·戴西（Mike Daisy），他以在舞台上表演独角戏为生。

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.200
He's been doing that for years.
他已经做这个很多年了。

00:01:53.260 --> 00:02:01.540
Though you're going to hear in this story that he turns himself into an amateur reporter during the course of the story, using some investigative techniques once he gets going.
虽然你在接下来的故事里会听到，他在故事进行的过程中把自己变成了一个业余记者，一旦行动起来，就运用了一些调查技巧。

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.260
I think very few reporters would ever try.
我想很少有记者会去尝试。

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.780
And finding lots of stuff I hadn't heard or seen anywhere else.
并且发现了许多我在其他任何地方都没有听说过或见过的事情。

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:09.660
Not like this.
从没有这样过。

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.460
From WBZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International.
来自芝加哥 WBZ 电台，这是《此生美国》（This American Life），由公共广播国际公司分发播出。

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:15.810
I'm Ira Glass.
我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.320
Today on our program, Mike Daisy takes us where Siri fears to tread.
今天在我们的节目中，迈克·戴西将带我们去 Siri 不敢涉足的地方。

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:27.660
What you're about to hear is an excerpt of Mike Daisy's show, which he adapted for the radio and performed for a small audience.
你即将听到的是迈克·戴西节目的片段，他为广播进行了改编并为一小群观众进行了表演。

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.120
Here is Mike Daisy.
这就是迈克·戴西。

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.880
My only hobby is technology.
我唯一的爱好是科技。

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.080
I love technology.
我热爱科技。

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.960
I love everything about it.
我热爱关于它的一切。

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.080
I love looking at technology.
我热爱研究科技。

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.520
I love comparing one piece of technology with another.
我热爱把一件科技产品和另一件进行对比。

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.760
I love reading rumors about technology that doesn't exist yet.
我热爱阅读关于尚未问世的科技产品的传闻。

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.160
I love browsing technology.
我热爱浏览科技。

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.160
I love buying technology.
我热爱购买科技。

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.720
I love opening technology.
我热爱开箱科技产品。

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.180
Even when it's in that bubble packaging, I love opening it.
即使是那种泡罩包装，我也热爱打开它。

00:02:53.260 --> 00:03:03.740
I love the smell of a new piece of technology, that sort of burnt PVC smell when you run electricity through it for the first time.
我热爱新科技产品的那种气味，当你第一次给它通电时那种类似烧焦的 PVC 的气味。

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:05.400
I love that.
我热爱那个。

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:19.060
And of all the kinds of technology that I love in the world, I love the technology that comes from Apple the most, because I am an Apple aficionado.
而在我热爱的世界上所有种类的科技中，我最爱苹果的科技，因为我是苹果的狂热爱好者。

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:21.900
I'm an Apple partisan.
我是苹果的忠实拥趸。

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:23.450
I'm an Apple fanboy.
我是苹果的狂热粉丝。

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:24.340
I'm a worshiper.
我是个崇拜者。

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.620
In the cult of Mac, I have been to the House of Jobs.
在苹果教里，我去过乔布斯的殿堂。

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.400
I have walked through the stations of his cross.
我走过他受难的十字苦路。

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.420
I have knelt before his his throne.
我曾跪在他的宝座前。

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:43.120
And like so many of you who may be members of this religion with me, you may know that it can be difficult at times to keep the faith.
就像你们许多可能和我一样是这个宗教信徒的人一样，你们可能会知道，有时候要保持这份信仰是很困难的。

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.520
And I have strayed now and again.
我偶尔也会误入歧途。

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.800
In the late 90s, I did sleep with a Windows system or two.
在 90 年代末，我确实和一两个 Windows 系统睡过觉。

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:54.060
But who didn't, really?
但谁又没有呢，真的？

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.820
But for the most part, I have been faithful.
但大多数时候，我一直是忠诚的。

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:08.720
But I do think it's important to understand where I sit in that hierarchy of Apple geeks for the purposes of our story.
但我确实认为，为了我们故事的需要，了解我在苹果极客等级中的位置是很重要的。

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:28.680
And so the best way I know to describe it is to say that I am at the level of geekishness where, to relax, after performances like this one, sometimes I will go back to my apartment and I will field strip my MacBook Pro into its 43 component pieces.
所以我知道的最好的描述方式就是说，我达到了这样的极客水平：为了放松，在经历这样的表演之后，有时我会回到我的公寓，将我的 MacBook Pro 彻底拆解成 43 个零部件。

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:35.800
I will clean them with compressed air, and I will put them back together again.
我会用压缩空气清洁它们，然后再把它们重新组装起来。

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:41.100
It soothes me.
这能安慰我。

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:47.340
So the truth is, I never would have questioned this religion.
所以事实是，我绝不会质疑这个宗教。

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:53.960
I never would have looked deeply at this belief system because it gave me so much pleasure if it hadn't been for the pictures.
我绝不会深入审视这个信仰体系，因为它给我带来了如此多的快乐，如果不是因为那些照片的话。

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:04.440
Because one day I was relaxing on the internet, which for me means reading Macintosh news sites, which I should specify have no actual news in them.
因为有一天我在互联网上放松，对我和我来说这意味着阅读麦金塔（Macintosh）新闻网站，我应该说明一下，这些网站里面根本没有真正的新闻。

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:14.680
They're instead filled with rumors about what Apple will do next, written exclusively by people who have no goddamn idea what Apple will do next.
相反，它们充斥着关于苹果下一步会做什么的谣言，而这些谣言完全是由那些对苹果下一步会做什么一无所知的人写的。

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:18.300
But for some reason I find this soothing.
但不知为什么，我发现这很令人慰藉。

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:28.080
So I'm reading one of those news sites when this article gets posted, and it's about the fact that someone bought an iPhone and when they got it, it wasn't blank.
所以我在看其中一个新闻网站时，这篇文章被发布了，内容是关于有人买了一部 iPhone，当他们拿到手时，它不是空白的。

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:32.420
It had information on it from inside the factory.
上面有来自工厂内部的信息。

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:38.320
And in fact, in the camera roll, there were pictures on it from inside the factory.
事实上，在相册胶卷里，有来自工厂内部的照片。

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.460
And they posted these pictures into the article.
他们把这些照片发到了文章里。

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.020
And I looked at these pictures and they took my breath away.
我看着这些照片，它们让我感到窒息。

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.100
They're not very good pictures.
它们并不是很好的照片。

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.740
They're just testing that that the camera on the phone works.
它们只是在测试手机上的摄像头是否正常工作。

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.820
They're not of anything, but I'll never forget them.
它们拍的什么都不是，但我永远不会忘记它们。

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:57.940
There were four of them.
一共有四张。

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:07.580
First, there was a stack of pallets, wooden pallets stacked up, and the second one was the edge of a conveyor belt.
首先，是一堆托盘，木制托盘堆叠在一起，第二张是一个传送带的边缘。

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.120
And the third was totally out of focus.
第三张完全失焦了。

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.840
It could just be an enormous space.
那可能只是一个巨大的空间。

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.580
And the fourth was a woman.
第四张是一个女人。

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.040
She doesn't know her picture's being taken.
她不知道自己被拍照了。

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.400
and she's looking off in another direction.
她正看向另一个方向。

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:23.980
She's wearing a clean suit.
她穿着无尘服。

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:27.380
She has no expression on her face.
她脸上没有任何表情。

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:45.580
And I looked at these pictures and I downloaded these pictures to my desktop and I put them in a folder on my desktop and in the weeks and months that followed, I found myself returning to them again and again, almost compulsively.
我看着这些照片，把它们下载到我的桌面，把它们放在桌面上的一个文件夹里，在接下来的几周和几个月里，我发现自己一遍又一遍地回到它们身边，几乎带有强迫性。

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.580
I would mouse over there and I would fan them across my desktop.
我会把鼠标移到那里，把它们在我的桌面上扇形展开。

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.000
And I would look at them.
然后我会看着它们。

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.680
Who are these people?
这些人是谁？

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:08.560
Because you have to understand, I have dedicated an embarrassing amount of my life to the study of these machines.
因为你必须明白，我把生命中令人尴尬的大量时间奉献给了对这些机器的研究。

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.980
I'm an amateur, but I'm a dedicated amateur.
我是个业余爱好者，但我是一个专一的业余爱好者。

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:17.720
I understand as best I can how the hardware works and how the software rests on the hardware.
我尽可能地理解硬件是如何工作的，以及软件是如何依附于硬件的。

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:27.780
And all that time, until I saw those pictures, it was only then I realized I had never thought ever, in a dedicated way, about how they were made.
而所有那些时间，直到我看到那些照片，我才意识到，我从来没有以一种专注的方式思考过它们是如何制造的。

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.240
It's actually hard now to reconstruct what I did think.
现在其实很难重建我当时的想法。

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.680
I think what I thought is they were made by robots.
我想我当时的想法是它们是由机器人制造的。

00:07:41.040 --> 00:08:04.080
like I had an image in my mind that I now realize I just stole from a 60 minute story about Japanese automotive plants I just copy and pasted that I was like, plop, command V, plop it looks like that but smaller because they're laptops tops.
就像我脑海里有一个画面，我现在意识到我只是从一个关于日本汽车厂的 60 分钟新闻报道里偷来的，我只是复制粘贴，我想象着，啪，Command V，啪，它看起来就像那样，只是因为它们是笔记本电脑，所以更小一些。

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:23.420
I started to think how if this phone has four pictures on it taken by hand in testing, then every iPhone has four pictures on it taken in testing every iPhone in the world by hand.
我开始思考，如果这部手机上有四张在测试中手动拍摄的照片，那么每部 iPhone 上都有四张在测试中拍摄的照片，世界上每一部 iPhone 都是人工拍摄的。

00:08:25.060 --> 00:09:01.460
I started to think, and that's always a problem for any religion the moment when you begin to think shenzhen is a city without history the people who live there will tell you that because 31 years ago shenzhen was a small town a little reed huts little reed walkways between the huts the men would fish in the late afternoon.
我开始思考，而这对于任何宗教来说都是一个问题——当你开始思考的那个时刻：深圳是一个没有历史的城市，住在那里的人会告诉你这一点，因为 31 年前，深圳还是一个小镇，茅草屋搭成的小路在茅草屋之间穿梭，男人们在傍晚钓鱼。

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.140
I hear it was lovely.
我听说那里很可爱。

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:08.320
Today, Shenzhen is a city of 14 million people.
今天，深圳是一个拥有 1400 万人口的城市。

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:12.000
It is larger than New York City, depending on how you count it.
比纽约市还要大，这取决于你如何计算。

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:19.400
It's the third largest city in all of China and is the place where almost all of your crap comes from.
它是全中国第三大城市，也是你几乎所有的垃圾（商品）产出的地方。

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:26.960
And the most amazing thing is, almost no one in America knows its name.
最神奇的是，美国几乎没有人知道它的名字。

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:29.590
Isn't that remarkable?
这不是很奇妙吗？

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:36.400
That there's a city where almost all of our crap comes from and no one knows its name?
有一个城市，我们几乎所有的垃圾都来自那里，而没有人知道它的名字？

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.420
I mean, we think we do know where our crap comes from.
我的意思是，我们以为我们知道我们的垃圾来自哪里。

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:41.960
We're not ignorant.
我们并非无知。

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:44.220
We think our crap comes comes from China.
我们以为我们的垃圾来自中国。

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:47.740
Right?
对吧？

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:50.000
Kind of a generalized way.
一种比较笼统的方式。

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:52.180
China.
中国。

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.410
But it doesn't come from China.
但它不是来自中国。

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:58.860
It comes from Shenzhen.
它来自深圳。

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:00.340
It's a city.
它是一个城市。

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.120
It's a place.
它是一个地方。

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:05.960
And I am there in an elevator going down to the lobby of my hotel to meet with my translator, Kathy.
而我就在那里，坐着电梯下到酒店大堂去见我的翻译凯茜。

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:09.220
Kathy is fascinating.
凯茜非常迷人。

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.260
She's very small.
她个子很小。

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.130
She has these sort of rounded shoulders, and she has these glasses.
她有着那种微微耸起的肩膀，还戴着一副眼镜。

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.670
that are way too big for her face, so they keep sliding down, and she has to push them up assiduously.
那副眼镜对她的脸来说太大了，所以它们一直在往下滑，她不得不勤奋地把它们推上去。

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:25.880
She also has this sort of unnerving habit that when she is listening to you, she leans forward indeterminately.
她还有一种让人不安的习惯，那就是当她听你说话时，她会不由自主地向前倾身。

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:42.160
So you really get the feeling that if you were to talk to her for long enough, she would actually fall into your chest, and you'd have to pick her back up again.
所以你真的会有一种感觉，如果你跟她说话的时间足够长，她其实会倒进你的怀里，而你不得不把她再扶起来。

00:10:42.680 --> 00:11:39.520
we go outside and get into a taxi and begin to drive to the streets of downtown Shenzhen Shenzhen looks like Blade Runner threw up on itself LEDs, neon and 15 story high video walls covered in ugly Chinese advertising it's everything they promised us the future would be we get out to the edge of the core of Shenzhen and we come to the gates because 31 years ago when Deng Xiaoping carved this area off from the rest of China with a big red pen he said this will be the special economic zone and he made a deal with the corporations he said listen use our people do whatever you want to our people just give us a modern China and the corporations took that deal and they squeezed and they squeezed and what they They got, is the Shenzhen we find today.
