I hand it to Kathy to translate.
我把它递给凯西翻译。

00:37:26.420 --> 00:37:32.100
You know, in a fascist country run by thugs, you don't have to be subtle.
你知道，在一个由暴徒统治的法西斯国家，你不需要表现得委婉。

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:36.680
You can say exactly what you mean.
你可以直言不讳。

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:41.120
The sheet is very clear that it comes from the labor board.
那张表格非常清楚地显示它来自劳动部门。

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:45.000
It says right across the top, the following is a list of troublemakers.
它在最顶端写着，以下是惹是生非者名单。

00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:47.880
If any of them are found in your Your employee dismissed them immediately.
如果在你们的员工中发现他们中的任何人，请立即予以解雇。

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:56.380
Then there's just column after column after column of names, page after page after page of them.
接下来是一列又一列的名字，一页又一页的名字。

00:37:57.160 --> 00:38:00.940
Kathy's hand trembles as she translates it.
凯西在翻译时手在颤抖。

00:38:03.860 --> 00:38:07.100
I talk to an older man with leathery skin.
我采访了一位皮肤像皮革似的老人。

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:10.060
His right hand is twisted up into a claw.
他的右手扭曲成了一个爪子。

00:38:10.220 --> 00:38:13.170
It was crushed in a metal press at Foxconn.
它在富士康的一台金属冲压机里被压碎了。

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:17.340
He says he didn't receive any medical attention and it healed this way.
他说他没有得到任何医疗救治，就这样长好了。

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:20.420
And then when he was too slow, they fired him.
然后当他动作变慢时，他们把他解雇了。

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:23.320
Today, he works at a woodworking plant.
如今，他在一家木制品厂工作。

00:38:23.480 --> 00:38:24.780
He says he likes it better.
他说他更喜欢这里。

00:38:24.880 --> 00:38:28.920
He says the people are nicer and the hours are more reasonable.
他说这里的人更好，工时也更合理。

00:38:29.260 --> 00:38:31.580
He works about 70 hours a week.
他每周工作大约70个小时。

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:35.540
And I ask him what he did when he was at Foxconn.
我问他以前在富士康做什么。

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:39.180
And he says he worked on the metal enclosures for the laptops.
他说他负责笔记本电脑的金属外壳。

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:41.600
And he worked on the iPad.
他还负责iPad。

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:48.460
And when he says this, I reach into my satchel and I take out my iPad.
当他说这话时，我把手伸进背包，拿出了我的iPad。

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:59.000
And when he sees it, his eyes widen because one of the ultimate ironies of globalism, at this point, there are no iPads in China.
当他看到它时，眼睛睁得老大，因为全球化的终极讽刺之一在于，此时此刻，中国并没有iPad。

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:10.840
Even though every last one that was made at factories in China, they've all been packaged up in perfectly minimalist Apple packaging and then shipped across the seas so that we can all enjoy them.
尽管中国工厂制造了每一个iPad，但它们都被装进完美极简主义的苹果包装中，然后远渡重洋，好让我们可以尽情享受。

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:11.060
him.
他。

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:18.340
He's never actually seen one on, this thing that took his hand.
他实际上从未见过开机的实物，这个夺走他双手的东西。

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:25.180
I turn it on, unlock the screen, and pass it to him.
我打开它，解锁屏幕，并把它递给他。

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:26.980
He takes it.
他接了过去。

00:39:27.160 --> 00:39:36.580
The icons flare into view, and he strokes the screen with his ruined hand, and the icons slide back and forth.
图标闪亮登场，他用残疾的手抚摸着屏幕，图标随之左右滑动。

00:39:37.340 --> 00:39:40.080
And he says something to Kathy.
他对凯西说了一句话。

00:39:40.660 --> 00:39:48.500
And Kathy says, he says, it's a kind of magic.
凯西说，他说，这是一种魔法。

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:53.500
It's a long day.
这是漫长的一天。

00:39:54.150 --> 00:39:58.600
At the end of it, I'm packing up everything to go, and Kathy says something to me.
结束时，我正在收拾行李准备离开，凯西突然对我说了一句话。

00:39:58.640 --> 00:40:29.720
Out of nowhere, where she says do you think these people are mentally ill do you think it is possible they are making all this up and i look at her as though for the first time because i mean let's be clear she's my chinese worker i pay her for her time i don't think about her very much at all but But now, I really look at her.
毫无预兆地，她说你觉得这些人精神有问题吗，你觉得他们有可能是在编造这一切吗？我看着她，仿佛那是第一次真正看她，因为我是说，坦白讲，她是我的中国员工，我付给她工钱，我其实不太在意她，但现在，我真的在审视她。

00:40:31.720 --> 00:40:40.960
She is exactly who all these workers I've been talking to for weeks, she is exactly what they're all dreaming that their children will one day be.
她正是那些我几周来一直在交谈的工人们的缩影，她正是他们所有人梦想着自己的孩子有一天能成为的样子。

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:46.320
She has a good life in the center of Shenzhen for her, for her family.
她在深圳中心区过着不错的生活，为了她自己，也为了她的家人。

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:49.840
What does this look like to her?
这一切在她眼里是什么样的？

00:40:52.300 --> 00:40:55.260
I say to her, what do you think?
我对她说，你觉得呢？

00:40:57.280 --> 00:40:59.000
do you think they're mentally ill?
你觉得他们精神有问题吗？

00:41:01.220 --> 00:41:04.380
And she suddenly looks very tired.
她突然显得非常疲惫。

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:20.880
She takes off her glasses and she rubs the bridge of her nose and she says, no, I do not think they are mentally ill.
她摘下眼镜，揉了揉鼻梁说，不，我不认为他们精神有问题。

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:56.560
It's just that You hear stories But you do not think it is going to be so much You know It's just so much And I reach across the table And I touch her hand It's the first and last time we will ever touch I am this woman whose real name I don't even know I say to her I know exactly what you mean.
只是你听过各种故事，但你没想到事情会这么多，你知道的，真的是太多了，我把手伸过桌子，触摸她的手，这是我们唯一一次也是最后一次触摸，我是这个连真名我都不知道的女人，我对她说，我完全明白你的意思。

00:42:22.300 --> 00:42:23.140
when our program continues.
节目稍后继续。

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:27.880
This is American Life with Myra Glass.
这里是迈拉·格拉斯（Myra Glass）主持的《美国生活》。

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:31.320
Today on our program, Mr. Daisy goes to the Apple factory.
今天我们的节目是：迈克·达西（Mike Daisy）走进苹果工厂。

00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:36.860
We've arrived at Act 2 of our show, in which our subject is actually Act 1 of our show.
我们来到了节目的第二幕，在这一幕中，我们的采访对象实际上正是我们节目的第一幕。

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:40.040
And so, Act 2 of our show, Act 1.
所以，这是节目的第二幕，关于第一幕。

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:45.980
When I saw Mike Daisy perform this story on stage, when I left the theater, I had a lot of questions.
当我看到迈克·达西在舞台上表演这个故事时，当我离开剧院时，我心中有很多疑问。

00:42:46.550 --> 00:42:50.020
He's not a reporter, and I wondered, did he get it right?
他不是记者，我在想，他弄对了吗？

00:42:50.020 --> 00:42:50.180
Right.
没错。

00:42:51.080 --> 00:42:54.580
And so we've actually spent a few weeks checking everything that he says in his show.
所以我们其实花了好几个星期去核实他在节目中所说的一切。

00:42:55.160 --> 00:42:58.420
We invited Apple to come onto the program and respond, and they turned us down.
我们邀请苹果公司来节目中回应，但他们拒绝了。

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:02.700
We invited Foxconn to come onto the program and respond, and they also said no.
我们邀请富士康来节目中回应，他们也拒绝了。

00:43:03.230 --> 00:43:08.740
Mike, however, was willing to come in and explain his methods at Foxconn's gates and in the factories that he visited.
不过，迈克愿意来到节目中，解释他在富士康大门外以及他参观过的工厂里的调查方法。

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:15.760
I talked to about 100 workers, a little over 100, over a number of different days outside the gates.
我在大门外断断续续的花了好几天时间，跟大约100名，稍微多一点100名工人交谈过。

00:43:15.760 --> 00:43:21.960
And I went to about 10 different factories when I was posing.
而且我伪装的时候去大约10家不同的工厂。

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:25.240
When you met with the union workers, how many of those did you meet with?
当你与工会工人会面时，你和其中多少人见过面？

00:43:25.780 --> 00:43:26.400
There were three of them.
有三个人。

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:28.780
And then the workers who came through to meet you?
那后来来见你的工人们呢？

00:43:29.280 --> 00:43:34.380
God, there were like 25, 30 throughout the course of the day.
天哪，一整天下来大概有25到30个。

00:43:35.220 --> 00:43:39.680
As for Mike's findings, we have gone through his script and fact -checked everything that was checkable.
至于迈克的调查结果，我们翻阅了他的剧本，并对所有可核实的内容进行了事实核查。

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:45.640
In one instance, we think that his translator may have misunderstood or mistranslated a fact for Mike.
在一个例子中，我们认为他的翻译可能误解或错译了迈克听到的一个事实。

00:43:45.700 --> 00:43:50.740
He says in his show that workers told him that the cafeterias at Foxconn seat 10,000 people.
他在节目中说，工人们告诉他，富士康的食堂能容纳1万人。

00:43:51.380 --> 00:43:58.000
But based on press accounts, we think that it's possible that they serve 10,000 people but seat only 4,000 at a time.
但根据媒体报道，我们认为他们服务的可能是1万人，但同时只能容纳4000人就座。

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:00.860
Foxconn wouldn't answer the question for us directly.
富士康没有直接回答我们的问题。

00:44:02.260 --> 00:44:07.040
When it comes to the suicide rate at Foxconn, there were about 12 suicides at the Shenzhen plant in 2010.
说到富士康的自杀率，2010年深圳工厂大约发生了12起自杀事件。

00:44:07.500 --> 00:44:09.260
It's actually hard to get the exact number.
要得到确切数字其实很难。

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:18.060
Some people have pointed out that 12 suicides for 400,000 workers is actually much lower than China's suicide rate as a whole.
有些人指出，40万工人中有12起自杀，实际上远低于中国整体的自杀率。

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:23.380
China has an unusually high suicide rate of 22 suicides per year per 100,000 people.
中国的自杀率异常之高，每10万人中有22起。

00:44:24.160 --> 00:44:27.520
That would work out to 88 suicides for 400,000 workers.
换算下来，40万工人应该有88起自杀。

00:44:28.440 --> 00:44:32.440
Mike Daisy points out that we don't actually know if these are the only suicides at Foxconn.
迈克·达西指出，我们实际上并不知道这些是否是富士康全部的自杀事件。

00:44:33.420 --> 00:44:38.840
And the biggest problem is that it isn't the quantity, it's the cluster.
最大的问题不在于数量，而在于聚集性。

00:44:38.840 --> 00:44:54.500
You know, if there was any company in America where a sizable chunk of your workforce went up over a period of time, especially close to one another, and killed themselves in the same way very publicly, it would be an enormous news story because it's far outside the norm.
你知道的，如果在美国的任何一家公司，相当一部分员工在一段时间内接连离开，尤其是彼此非常接近，并且以同样非常公开的方式自杀，那将成为一条轰动的新闻，因为它严重超出了常态。

00:44:55.040 --> 00:44:57.260
Overall, we checked with over a dozen people.
总的来说，我们向十几个人进行了核实。

00:44:57.860 --> 00:45:07.960
There would be journalists who cover these factories, people who work with the electronics industry in China, activists, labor groups, about the working conditions that Mike Daisy describes in his show.
其中包括报道这些工厂的记者、在中国与电子行业打交道的人、维权人士、劳工团体，向他们求证了迈克·达西在节目中描述的工作条件。

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:10.160
And nobody seemed very surprised by them.
似乎没有人对这些情况感到十分惊奇。

00:45:10.420 --> 00:45:16.040
Well, unfortunately, I think some of these conditions sound actually quite common.
嗯，不幸的是，我认为其中一些情况听起来确实相当普遍。

00:45:16.640 --> 00:45:21.960
This is Ian Spalding, who estimates that he has been in or worked with about 1,000 factories throughout China.
这是伊恩·斯伯丁（Ian Spalding），他估计自己曾进入或合作过中国大约1000家工厂。

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:31.750
The company that he founded and runs, In Fact Global Partners, goes into Chinese factories and helps them meet social responsibility standards that are set by Western companies.
他创立并运营的公司——“事实全球合伙人”（In Fact Global Partners）会深入中国工厂，帮助他们达到西方公司制定的社会责任标准。

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:36.100
So those companies are ready when outside auditors come and check on working conditions.
这样一来，当外部审核员前来检查工作条件时，这些公司就能做好准备。

00:45:36.820 --> 00:45:40.320
He has a staff of 45 that do hundreds of factories a year, including electronics.
他拥有45名员工，每年审核数百家工厂，包括电子厂。

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:46.940
There are hours in factories that are often too long and are excessive and required over time.
工厂里的工时往往过长、超标，并且是强制加班的。

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:47.280
time.
加班。

00:45:47.800 --> 00:45:55.500
Things like cramped quarters can also happen, and repetitive motion injuries are also can be quite common.
诸如居住环境拥挤之类的情况也会发生，重复性劳损也相当普遍。

00:45:56.500 --> 00:46:02.960
Another thing that Mike Daisy says that's disturbing to hear is he says that the companies will deceive the auditors when the auditors come in.
迈克·达西提到的另一件令人不安的事情是，他说当审核员到来时，公司会欺骗审核员。

00:46:03.660 --> 00:46:04.180
Have you seen that?
你见过这种情况吗？

00:46:04.600 --> 00:46:09.700
Yeah, that actually is quite common, and I think many other people have also exposed this problem.
是的，这其实相当普遍，而且我认为许多其他人也已经揭露过这个问题。

00:46:10.560 --> 00:46:11.400
Now, don't get the wrong impression.
现在，别产生错误的印象。

00:46:11.980 --> 00:46:14.720
Ian Spalding did have a few quibbles with Mike Daisy.
伊恩·斯伯丁对迈克·达西确实有一些小异议。

00:46:15.100 --> 00:46:20.080
He said that if a worker gets injured and then is fired by his company, he or she can sue the company.
他说，如果工人受伤然后被公司解雇，他或她是可以起诉公司的。

00:46:20.270 --> 00:46:22.360
And he said that lots of people are doing that these days.
他说现在有很多人都在这么做。

00:46:22.740 --> 00:46:26.360
He said electronics companies have been improving their handling of toxic chemicals.
他说电子企业一直在改进对有毒化学品的处理。

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:32.560
And his only real objection to anything that Mike Daisy found had to do with child labor.
他对迈克·达西发现的任何情况唯一真正的反对意见与童工有关。

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:39.500
Ian Spalding said, yes, there definitely is child labor in China, but not at the top tier electronics manufacturers.
伊恩·斯伯丁说，是的，中国绝对存在童工，但不会在一流的电子产品制造商那里。

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:42.440
Other people who we talked to who agreed with this.
我们交谈过的其他人也认同这一点。

00:46:42.820 --> 00:46:46.840
Even people who are critical of Foxconn for all kinds of things agreed with this.
甚至那些在各种事情上批评富士康的人也认同这一点。

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:52.400
They said maybe a stray worker here and there might get in on a borrowed ID, but it is not a widespread problem.
他们说，也许偶尔会有一个零星的工人借用身份证混进来，但这并不是一个普遍存在的问题。

00:46:53.040 --> 00:46:54.800
Well, I don't know if it's a big problem.
嗯，我不知道这算不算是个大问题。

00:46:55.000 --> 00:46:56.200
I just know that I saw it.
我只知道我亲眼看到了。

00:46:56.460 --> 00:46:57.340
Again, Mike Daisy.
再说一次，这是迈克·戴西说的。

00:46:57.820 --> 00:46:59.500
He says, sure, maybe it's not prevalent.
他说，当然，也许这并不普遍。

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:04.060
I know that I met people that were there and I know that I that I talked to them.
我知道我遇到了在那里的人，我知道我跟他们交谈过。

00:47:04.540 --> 00:47:08.980
I mean, there weren't very many as a proportion of the total group.
我的意思是，相对于整个群体来说，人数并不是很多。

00:47:09.100 --> 00:47:10.740
I talked to more than 100 people.
我交谈过的人超过100个。

00:47:10.880 --> 00:47:12.980
I met five or six who were under age.
我遇到了五六个未成年人。

00:47:13.240 --> 00:47:15.440
And they were over the course of days?
而且他们是在好几天内遇到的吗？

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:17.500
No, they were together in a group.
不，他们是一起在一个群体里的。

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:23.200
So it's basically the girl who you describe who deals with the iPhones, who wipes off your thing, and then her friends.
所以基本上就是你描述的那个处理iPhone、擦拭你的设备的女孩子，还有她的朋友们。

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:24.880
And then some people that were with her.
然后还有一些跟她在一起的人。

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:27.960
They seemed like savvy kids, honestly.
说实话，他们看起来像是精明的小孩。

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:33.260
The one source that I could find that backs up Mike on this one, at least a little bit, is Apple.
我能找到的一个至少在一定程度上支持迈克说法的消息来源，就是苹果公司。

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:45.700
Apple has released a report stating in the year that Mike was in China, 2010, Apple's own auditors went into 127 facilities around the world that make its products and say they found 91 underage workers.
苹果发布了一份报告称，在迈克去中国的2010年，苹果自己的审核员走访了全球127家制造其产品的工厂，并表示他们发现了91名未成年工人。

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:48.140
It doesn't say which facilities the workers were at.
报告没有说明这些工人在哪些工厂。

00:47:48.740 --> 00:48:06.220
The report states that Apple helped install systems to verify ages, educated suppliers on recruiting practices, made them return underage workers to school and made them pay pay for the kids' education, and then it stopped doing business with one supplier that had 42 underage workers and showed no commitment to addressing the problem.
报告称，苹果协助安装了验证年龄的系统，向供应商普及招聘规范，让他们将未成年工人送回学校并支付孩子们的教育费用，随后苹果停止与一家拥有42名未成年工人且对解决该问题表现出毫无诚意的供应商合作。

00:48:12.660 --> 00:48:19.700
All this research that we did did fill in some interesting details about working conditions at Foxconn that are not in Mike Daisy's show.
我们做所有这些调查确实补充了一些关于富士康工作条件、但迈克·戴西的演出中没有提到的有趣细节。

00:48:20.380 --> 00:48:25.520
There's an advocacy group called SACOM, which is Students and Scholars Against Corporate Misbehavior.
有一个名为SACOM的维权组织，即“大学师生监察无良企业行动”。

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:26.660
They're based in Hong Kong.
他们的总部设在香港。

00:48:26.660 --> 00:48:34.100
And since the suicides at Foxconn in 2010, they have put out three reports investigating conditions at the company.
自2010年富士康发生员工坠楼事件以来，他们已经发布了三份调查该企业状况的报告。

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:41.120
Each report surveyed over 100 Foxconn workers and even had a researcher go undercover and take a job at the Shenzhen plant.
每份报告都调查了100多名富士康工人，甚至派出一名研究人员卧底并到深圳工厂工作。

00:48:42.020 --> 00:48:44.200
Debbie Chan-Si Wen is a project manager at Satcom.
陈诗韵是SACOM的项目经理。

00:48:44.380 --> 00:48:50.100
The surveys that she did of workers depict a company where many employees have to stand all day long.
她对工人们进行的调查勾勒出这样一家公司：许多员工不得不整天站着上班。

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:58.620
Because there is some research in the industry that workers who stand during work is more efficient than those who sit.
因为行业内有一些研究表明，站着工作的工人比坐着的工人效率更高。

00:48:59.420 --> 00:49:04.580
And workers, they are regularly yelled at by the supervisors.
而且工人们经常遭到主管的训斥。

00:49:04.760 --> 00:49:09.420
And if they make some mistake, then they have to write confession letters, etc.
如果他们犯了什么错，就必须写悔过书等。

00:49:10.460 --> 00:49:14.300
In your documents, you call this military-style management.
在你们的文件中，你们称这为军事化管理。

00:49:15.220 --> 00:49:16.100
Why is that what you call it?
为什么你们会这么叫？

00:49:17.280 --> 00:49:21.320
I think it is the word given by some of the workers.
我想这是工人们自己所用的词。

00:49:22.920 --> 00:49:28.760
According to Satcom's surveys and reports, the wages at Foxconn are not much more than the Chinese minimum wage.
根据SACOM的调查和报告，富士康的工资并不比中国最低工资高出多少。

00:49:29.720 --> 00:49:35.740
Satcom claims that it's only with tons of overtime that the money approaches what Satcom calculates as a decent living wage for a family.
SACOM称，只有通过大量的加班，薪水才能接近SACOM计算的家庭体面生活所需的工资水平。

00:49:35.850 --> 00:49:46.220
But probably the most surprising thing I learned about Foxconn and other Chinese electronics manufacturers from Debbie and from Ian Spalding had to do with the turnover rate.
但从黛比和伊恩·斯波尔丁那里了解到的关于富士康和其他中国电子产品制造商的最令人惊讶的事情，可能与员工流失率有关。

00:49:46.900 --> 00:49:51.040
Ian Spalding says that it could be 10 to 20 percent turnover per month.
伊恩·斯波尔丁说，每月的员工流失率可能高达10%到20%。

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:54.240
He says it's a huge business problem these days in China.
他说，这如今是中国的一个巨大的商业难题。

00:49:54.760 --> 00:50:04.840
So you imagine the number of employees that you're hiring and that leave after one week, two week, one month on the job, and you're constantly trying to rehire people into those positions.
所以你可以想象，你雇用了大量员工，但他们在工作了一周、两周或一个月后就离职了，而你不得不 constamment 试图重新招人来填补这些空缺。

00:50:05.840 --> 00:50:15.210
With so many workers quitting, why doesn't that lead to companies changing conditions and raising salaries so they don't have to go through the hassle of hiring new people?
既然有这么多工人辞职，为什么这没有促使企业改善条件、提高薪资，从而免去重新招人的麻烦呢？

00:50:16.360 --> 00:50:17.940
Well, that's the good news.
嗯，好消息就在这里。

00:50:18.200 --> 00:50:18.920
It is.
确实如此。

00:50:19.640 --> 00:50:24.220
Nowadays, a lot of people talk about what should companies, brands, U.S.
如今，许多人在讨论企业、品牌、美国品牌

00:50:24.220 --> 00:50:27.400
and European brands do to make conditions better.
以及欧洲品牌应该做些什么来改善条件。

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:37.560
And the reality is that actually what's proven to be more effective is this bottom-up labor market that's emerging where employees are speaking with their feet.
而现实是，实际上被证明更有效的是这种正在涌现的自下而上的劳动力市场，员工们正用脚投票。

00:50:37.640 --> 00:50:46.640
By leaving the factory, they're forcing factories to improve wages, improve working conditions, and improving dormitories to make things more attractive for employees.
通过离开工厂，他们迫使工厂提高工资、改善工作条件并改善宿舍，以吸引员工。

00:50:50.700 --> 00:51:00.620
When Apple turned down our invitation to come onto today's radio show, in a rather Orwellian gesture, they told us that they are 100% transparent as they refuse to come on the air.
当苹果拒绝我们邀请其参加今天的广播节目时，他们采取了一种颇具奥威尔风格的姿态，在拒绝上节目的同时告诉我们他们是百分之百透明的。

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:08.400
They refer to these reports that they've been issuing every year since 2007 on working conditions of the factories that make their products overseas.
他们提到了自2007年以来每年发布的关于海外产品代工厂工作条件的报告。

00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:11.120
And these reports, I have to say, are remarkable documents.
我必须说，这些报告是非常了不起的文件。

00:51:11.380 --> 00:51:13.340
You can find these online at Apple's website.
你可以在苹果官网上找到这些内容。

00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:20.220
Apple, like many companies, has a code of conduct that suppliers have to commit to before they can do business with them.
苹果和许多公司一样，有一套供应商在与其开展业务前必须承诺遵守的行为准则。

00:51:20.820 --> 00:51:24.660
And each year, Apple audits many of the suppliers to make sure that they are complying with the code.
每年，苹果都会对许多供应商进行审核，以确保他们遵守该准则。

00:51:25.080 --> 00:51:29.960
If they don't, then there are corrective action plans and there's training and there's follow-up audits.
如果不遵守，就会有纠正措施计划、培训和后续审核。

00:51:29.960 --> 00:51:35.880
It's very elaborate, and if it all fails, Apple stops buying from the supplier, or that's what they claim anyway.
这非常详尽，如果这一切都失败了，苹果就会停止向该供应商采购，或者他们是这么声称的。

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:41.380
Apple monitors pretty much all the working conditions that Mike Daisy talks about in his show.
苹果几乎监控了迈克·戴西在他的节目中谈到的所有工作条件。

00:51:42.040 --> 00:51:47.940
The report covering the period that Mike was in China talks about what Apple did in the wake of the suicides at Foxconn.
涵盖迈克在中国期间的那份报告，谈到了苹果在富士康员工坠楼事件后所采取的行动。

00:51:48.460 --> 00:51:50.110
They say they did an independent review.
他们表示进行了一次独立审查。

00:51:50.900 --> 00:51:55.480
They asked for mental health counselors and other changes, which Apple says Foxconn has implemented.
他们要求提供心理健康咨询师以及其他改进措施，苹果称富士康已经落实了这些措施。

00:51:56.180 --> 00:51:59.640
The report also has a whole section on n-hexane.
报告中还有一个专门关于正己烷的章节。

00:52:00.130 --> 00:52:05.040
Which workers, not from Foxconn, but from another plant, told Mike that they were exposed to.
迈克采访的并非富士康、而是另一家工厂的工人告诉他，他们曾接触过这种物质。

00:52:05.280 --> 00:52:06.320
And they talked about it in his show.
他在节目中谈到了这一点。

00:52:06.880 --> 00:52:12.560
Apple says that it found 137 workers had adverse health effects after exposure to N-hexane.
苹果表示，经调查发现有137名工人在接触正己烷后出现了不良健康反应。

00:52:13.240 --> 00:52:20.280
It says that the supplier using the stuff was told to stop using the chemical and has been audited since then to make sure it has happened.
报告称，使用这种物质的供应商被责令停止使用该化学品，此后该供应商接受了审核以确保这一指令得到落实。

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:22.880
Mike Daisy has read these reports.
迈克·戴西读过这些报告。

00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:25.240
I'm glad Apple does this.
我很高兴苹果这样做。

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:27.920
It's unfortunate more companies don't do it.
遗憾的是，没有更多的公司这样做。

00:52:27.920 --> 00:52:30.620
And I do respect them for doing it.
我确实为他们这样做而尊重他们。

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:37.860
But it doesn't change the fact that the situation on the ground, even in their own reports, is not good.
但这并不能改变这样一个事实：实地情况，即便是从他们自己的报告来看，也并不好。

00:52:37.940 --> 00:52:43.750
And then every year, the numbers are roughly the same in terms of people who are non-compliant with overtime.
而且每年，在加班违规的人数方面，数字大致都差不多。

00:52:43.860 --> 00:52:44.180
time.
时间。

00:52:44.360 --> 00:52:57.060
And I would say that in the 2010 report, Apple found that only 32 percent of suppliers that had audited followed its standard about working hours, though Apple doesn't name the companies that they audited in the report.
我想说的是，在2010年的报告中，苹果发现只有32%的接受审核的供应商遵守了其关于工作时间的标准，尽管苹果并没有在报告中点名他们审核过的公司。

00:52:58.060 --> 00:53:00.400
And I really question the wisdom of that.
我真的对这种做法的明智性提出质疑。

00:53:00.580 --> 00:53:13.500
I think that if they have a serious commitment to changing how things are done in the special economic zone in Shenzhen, then they would name those companies and those companies would begin to be held held responsible.
我认为，如果他们真的致力于改变深圳经济特区的工作方式，那么他们就会点名这些公司，从而使这些公司开始承担责任。

00:53:14.700 --> 00:53:19.660
As it is, Daisy says, Apple is basically saying, trust us, we're taking care of the problems.
照目前这样，戴西说，苹果基本上是在说，相信我们，我们在处理这些问题。

00:53:20.360 --> 00:53:24.180
But without supplier names, nobody can independently verify any of it.
但如果没有供应商的名字，任何人也无法对其中任何内容进行独立核实。

00:53:29.080 --> 00:53:36.380
Should we feel weird about the computers and phones we use, all the clothes that we wear that are made in faraway factories in Asia under harsh working conditions?
我们是否应该对我们使用的电脑和手机、以及在亚洲遥远工厂的恶劣工作条件下制造的所有我们穿的衣服感到奇怪？

00:53:37.460 --> 00:53:40.500
Leaving Mike Daisy aside for a second, that's the question that all this raises, right?
暂且不提迈克·戴西，这就是这一切所引发的问题，对吧？

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:46.800
And the mainstream view that you would hear from lots of economists would be, no, we shouldn't feel weird.
你从许多经济学家那里听到的主流观点会是，不，我们不应该感到奇怪。

00:53:47.720 --> 00:53:58.600
The famously liberal Paul Krugman, winner of the Nobel Prize for Economics, New York Times columnist, has argued that in places like Indonesia, terrible factories, far, far worse than anything you've heard about here today.
以自由主义闻名的保罗·克鲁格曼——诺贝尔经济学奖得主、《纽约时报》专栏作家——曾辩称，在印度尼西亚等地，存在着极其糟糕的工厂，比你今天在这里听到的任何事情都要糟糕得多、糟糕得多。

00:53:58.720 --> 00:54:00.040
They raised the economy.
它们促进了经济发展。

00:54:00.220 --> 00:54:01.220
They made everybody better off.
它们让每个人的生活都变得更好了。

00:54:01.540 --> 00:54:02.850
Here's a quote from Krugman.
这里引用一段克鲁格曼的话。

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:11.400
It is the indirect and unintended results of the actions of soulless multinationals and rapacious local entrepreneurs that is not an edifying spectacle.
冷酷无情的跨国公司和贪婪的当地企业家的行动，其间接和非预期的结果并不是一幅赏心悦目的景象。

00:54:12.440 --> 00:54:21.680
But no matter how base the motives of those involved, the result has been to move hundreds of millions of people from abject poverty to something still awful, but nonetheless significantly better.
但无论涉事者的动机多么卑劣，其结果是将数亿人从赤贫状态带到了依然糟糕、但无论如何已有显著改善的境地。

00:54:23.300 --> 00:54:38.240
Another person whose support for sweatshops may surprise you is one of Krugman's colleagues at the New York Times columnist, Nicholas Kristof, who was a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize for Commentary, quote, for his powerful columns that portrayed suffering among the developing world's often forgotten people.
另一位对血汗工厂的支持可能会让你感到惊讶的人，是克鲁格曼在《纽约时报》的专栏作家同事尼古拉斯·克里斯托夫，他曾入围普利策评论奖决赛，“因其强有力的专栏文章刻画了发展中国家那些常被遗忘的人们的苦难而受到引述”。

00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:41.260
Kristof spent years reporting in Asia.
克里斯托夫在亚洲进行了多年的报道。

00:54:41.360 --> 00:54:43.860
He co-wrote a book about the changes going on in China.
他合著了一本关于中国正在发生的变化的书。

00:54:43.860 --> 00:54:56.880
Well, I mean, if you look at Shenzhen, for example, and Guangdong, where Foxconn is, then there's no doubt that it's been a tremendous benefit, well, not only to southern China, but indeed to much of Asia.
嗯，我的意思是，如果你看看深圳和有富士康所在的广东，毫无疑问，这带来了巨大的好处，不仅对中国南方，对整个亚洲的大部分地区也是如此。

00:54:57.780 --> 00:55:03.890
It created massive employment opportunities, especially for young women, who frankly didn't have a lot of alternatives.
它创造了大量的就业机会，尤其是为那些坦率地说没有太多其他选择的年轻女性。

00:55:03.960 --> 00:55:08.640
That tended to give women more clout within families, within the community.
这往往赋予了女性在家庭和社区中更多的话语权。

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:08.820
community.
社区。

00:55:09.360 --> 00:55:14.850
My wife's ancestral village is in southern China, not too far from Foxconn.
我太太的祖籍村庄在中国南方，离富士康不远。

00:55:15.380 --> 00:55:23.500
And people in that village went from a really grim kind of lifestyle, basically in the rice paddies.
那个村庄的人们从一种非常严酷的生活方式——基本上就是在稻田里劳作——转变过来了。

00:55:23.660 --> 00:55:31.600
And for them, and indeed for many Chinese, the grimness of factors like Foxconn was better than the grimness of the rice paddies.
对他们来说，乃至对许多中国人来说，像富士康这样的工厂虽然严酷，但也比稻田里的严酷要好。

00:55:32.200 --> 00:55:35.580
There's a passage of your own writing that we emailed you before you came in this morning.
在你今天早晨来之前，我们通过电子邮件发给你一段你自己的文字。

00:55:35.720 --> 00:55:36.480
Did you get that?
你收到了吗？

00:55:37.030 --> 00:55:37.620
Yeah, I did.
是的，我收到了。

00:55:37.840 --> 00:55:38.660
Can I ask you to read?
我能请你读一下吗？

00:55:39.100 --> 00:55:39.800
Sure, absolutely.
当然，没问题。

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:46.500
So this is from a New York Times Magazine article that you wrote in 2000, and the article was called Two Cheers for Sweatshops.
这段话摘自你在2000年为《纽约时报杂志》写的一篇文章，文章名为《为血汗工厂叫好两声》。

00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:50.920
Fourteen years ago, we moved to Asia and began reporting there.
十四年前，我们移居亚洲并在那里开始做报道。

00:55:51.480 --> 00:55:54.440
Like most Westerners, we arrived in the region outraged at sweatshops.
像大多数西方人一样，我们抵达该地区时对血汗工厂感到愤怒。

00:55:55.200 --> 00:55:58.060
In time, though, we came to accept the view supported by most Asians.
不过，随着时间推移，我们开始接受大多数亚洲人支持的观点。

00:55:58.720 --> 00:56:28.840
In the years since our first conversations there, we've returned many So you're saying sweatshops are bad, but we should feel okay about it?
自从我们在那里进行初次对话以来的这些年里，我们多次重返……所以你是说血汗工厂很糟糕，但我们对此应该感到安心？

00:56:28.840 --> 00:56:29.340
it?
吗？

00:56:30.800 --> 00:56:38.260
Well, it's a very awkward thing to, you know, to defend sweatshops, if you will.
嗯，要为血汗工厂辩护，你知道，这确实是一件非常尴尬的事情。

00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:43.880
I mean, I think it's useful to be reminded about how grim the conditions are.
我的意思是，我认为提醒人们记住工作条件有多么严酷是有益的。

00:56:44.980 --> 00:56:56.240
But again, I just think that if you try to think how you can fight poverty most effectively, and what has fought it within than China, then I think sweatshops are a key part of that answer.
但话说回来，我只是认为，如果你试着去思考如何最有效地消除贫困，以及中国是如何消除贫困的，那么我认为血汗工厂是其中的一个关键答案。

00:56:56.980 --> 00:56:58.760
To that, Mike Daisy says, sure.
对此，迈克·戴西说，当然。

00:56:58.920 --> 00:56:59.680
He's heard that argument.
他听说过这种论点。

00:57:00.720 --> 00:57:04.920
Sweatshops are just a phase poor countries go through, a phase that all the industrialized countries already went through.
血汗工厂只是贫困国家经历的一个阶段，是所有工业化国家都已经历过的阶段。

00:57:06.080 --> 00:57:16.480
But he says, even given that, don't we have some basic obligation as outsiders coming in to treat the workers the way that our country has already agreed that workers should be treated?
但他说，即便如此，作为外来者，我们难道没有某种基本义务，以我们国家已经认同的应有方式去对待工人们吗？

00:57:17.100 --> 00:57:27.880
It may be true that over the long span of time, in 100 years, people will look back and be happy about how things went, but I don't think it's as clean as that.
从长远来看，在100年后，人们回首过去时可能会对事情的发展感到高兴，这或许是真的，但我不认为事情会这么单纯。

00:57:27.980 --> 00:57:30.920
I just want basic labor protections for people.
我只是希望人们能获得基本的劳动保护。

00:57:31.580 --> 00:57:36.740
That's entirely compatible with everything that has been flourishing in Shenzhen.
这与在深圳蓬勃发展的一切完全不冲突。

00:57:37.140 --> 00:57:43.520
It's absolutely compatible to have everything work as it has, but also people are rotated in their factories.
让一切照常运转的同时在工厂里实行轮岗，这绝对是不冲突的。

00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:54.900
It's entirely compatible to have things work as they are now, but there are also independent people outside of individual corporations that inspect without announcement to see that basic labor standards are covered.
让现状照常运作，同时在各个独立企业之外有独立人员进行不打招呼的检查，以确保基本的劳动标准得到落实，这也是完全不冲突的。

00:57:55.060 --> 00:57:56.360
It's a really basic thing.
这是一件非常基础的事情。

00:57:56.420 --> 00:57:58.760
It's a basic thing that we fought for in this country.
这是我们在国内争取过的基本权益。

00:57:59.380 --> 00:58:03.260
It took 100 years of labor struggles to get to a place where that happened for most workers.
为了让大多数工人获得这样的待遇，我们经历了长达100年的劳工斗争。

00:58:04.160 --> 00:58:09.340
Then we exported those jobs overseas and we didn't send the protections with them.
然后我们将这些工作岗位输出到海外，却把保护措施留在了国内。

00:58:10.200 --> 00:58:10.820
And it's not right.
这不对劲。

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:28.560
Mike Daisy, his one-man show about Apple is going back on stage this month in New York at the Public Theater.
迈克·戴西关于苹果的独角戏本月将在纽约公共剧院重返舞台。

00:58:28.680 --> 00:58:35.760
The full show has this entire other storyline about Steve Jobs that you'll have to buy a theater ticket if you want to hear it.
整场演出还有另一条关于史蒂夫·乔布斯的完整故事线，如果你想听的话，就得买张剧院门票了。

00:58:37.500 --> 00:59:07.880
¶¶ ¶ Out of sight, out of mind So the story goes Well, our program was produced today, as always, entirely on Apple Computers by Brian Reed and myself, with Alex Bloomberg, Ben Calhoun, Sarah Kanick, Jonathan Menjivar, Lisa Pollock, Robin Semyon, Alyssa Shipp, and Nancy Updike.
¶¶ ¶ 眼不见，心不烦，世事便是如此。好了，今天的节目和往常一样，完全是由布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）和我自己在苹果电脑上制作的，参与制作的还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Bloomberg）、本·卡尔霍恩（Ben Calhoun）、萨拉·卡尼克（Sarah Kanick）、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔（Jonathan Menjivar）、丽莎·波洛克（Lisa Pollock）、罗宾·塞米恩（Robin Semyon）、阿莉莎·希普（Alyssa Shipp）和南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）。

00:59:08.240 --> 00:59:18.880
Our senior producers, Julie Snyder, Seth Lind is our production manager, Emily Condon is our office manager, production help from Miki Meek and Matt Kilty, Scouting help from Elma Baker, music help from Damian Gray from Rob Geddes.
我们的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder），赛斯·林德（Seth Lind）是我们的制作经理，埃米利·康登（Emily Condon）是我们的办公室经理，米基·米克（Miki Meek）和马特·基尔蒂（Matt Kilty）提供了制作协助，埃尔玛·贝克（Elma Baker）提供了搜寻协助，达米安·格雷（Damian Gray）和罗布·格德斯（Rob Geddes）提供了音乐协助。

00:59:19.780 --> 00:59:24.740
Special thanks today to Paul Ruist, who recorded Mike Daisy for us at the Barrow Group Theater in New York City.
今天我们要特别感谢保罗·鲁ist（Paul Ruist），他在纽约巴罗集团剧院（Barrow Group Theater）为我们录制了迈克·戴西的节目。

00:59:24.900 --> 00:59:30.160
Thanks to Seth Barish and Porter Pickard, to Mike's director, Jean Michelle Gregory, and to Andrew Wood.
感谢赛斯·巴里什（Seth Barish）和波特·皮卡德（Porter Pickard），感谢迈克的导演让·米歇尔·格雷戈里（Jean Michelle Gregory），以及安德鲁·伍德（Andrew Wood）。

00:59:30.680 --> 00:59:36.260
Our website, where we have links to information on Apple and on Foxconn, thisamericanlife.org.
我们的网站是thisamericanlife.org，上面有关于苹果和富士康信息的链接。

00:59:37.180 --> 00:59:40.060
A note to our Australian listeners, yes, we are on the radio in Australia.
给我们的澳大利亚听众的一个提示：是的，我们在澳大利亚的广播里播出。

00:59:40.530 --> 00:59:45.260
I will be in Perth, Adelaide, and Brisbane this month, and there are still tickets available.
我这个月会去珀斯、阿德莱德和布里斯班，目前还有门票。

00:59:46.080 --> 00:59:48.620
This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
《美国生活》由公共广播国际公司发行。

00:59:49.910 --> 00:59:53.200
WBEZ Management Oversight for our program by our boss, Ms. Tori Malatia.
WBEZ的管理层对我们的节目进行监督，我们的老板是托里·马拉蒂亚女士。

00:59:53.560 --> 00:59:56.520
Tori, how much money do you make anyway?
托里，你到底赚多少钱？

00:59:56.900 --> 00:59:58.080
I'm not allowed to say.
我不能说。

00:59:58.980 --> 01:00:00.400
And how old are you exactly?
那你到底多大年纪？

01:00:01.260 --> 01:00:01.760
Good question.
好问题。

01:00:02.600 --> 01:00:04.080
Anything else I can do for you?
还有什么需要我效劳的吗？

01:00:04.940 --> 01:00:05.440
I'm Ira Glass.
我是伊拉·格拉斯。

01:00:06.000 --> 01:00:08.180
Back next week, more stories of This American Life.
下周同一时间，将带给您更多《美国生活》的故事。

01:00:15.240 --> 01:00:34.440
